      "problem": "If $r=1,$ then $\\frac{7r}{15}+\\frac{6}{5r}=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We replace $r$ with $1$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7r}{15}+\\frac{6}{5r} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\cdot 1}{15}+\\frac{6}{5\\cdot 1} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{6}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{15}+\\frac{18}{15} & = \\\\ \\frac{7+18}{15} & = \\\\ \\frac{25}{15} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
